    <div class="mf-help-content">
        <section id="big-picture" class="mf-panel">
            <h2>Big Picture</h2>
            <p>CMIForge is a configurable legal intake and conflicts platform. The core idea is simple: build intake forms, collect submissions, route those submissions through workflow, run conflict checks, and convert approved submissions into client and matter records.</p>
            <ol class="mf-help-steps">
                <li>Create or edit a form, then publish a version.</li>
                <li>Attach a workflow to the published form version.</li>
                <li>Users submit the form.</li>
                <li>Workflow tasks route to users or teams.</li>
                <li>Attachments, conflict searches, and notes stay tied to the submission or matter context.</li>
                <li>Approved submissions can be converted into client and matter records.</li>
                <li>Time can be recorded against matters, then reviewed in operational reports.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section id="dashboard" class="mf-panel">
            <h2>Dashboard</h2>
            <p>The dashboard is the operational landing page for each tenant. It now combines quick counts with chart-style views so a reviewer can see the intake pulse without opening five separate lists.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>The dashboard selector switches between <strong>Firm Admin</strong>, <strong>My Work</strong>, and <strong>Matter Partner</strong> views.</li>
                <li>Top cards show active forms, submissions, published versions, and recorded time.</li>
                <li>Submission and conflict donuts show the current status mix.</li>
                <li>Open-work bars show where workflow tasks are waiting.</li>
                <li>The recent-submission trend shows the last 14 days of intake activity.</li>
                <li>Plan limit meters show current users, matters, and clients against the active plan limits.</li>
                <li>My Work focuses on the signed-in user's assigned workflow tasks, team queue, recent submissions, time entries, and conflict escalations.</li>
                <li>Matter Partner focuses on matters where the signed-in user is lead partner, including pending time approvals, conflict attention, and related submissions.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="forms" class="mf-panel">
            <h2>Forms</h2>
            <p>Forms define the intake questions users answer. CMIForge stores form designs as versioned schemas so historical submissions still render against the exact form version that was used at the time.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Fill Form</strong> takes users to the form list so they can choose the right published intake form.</li>
                <li><strong>Create Form</strong> publishes the first version for a new form definition.</li>
                <li><strong>Edit Form</strong> publishes a new version instead of changing older submissions.</li>
                <li>Blank designer rows are ignored.</li>
                <li>Select, radio-style, and checkbox-style fields use static options for now.</li>
                <li>Client fields can let a user pick an existing client or type a new client name.</li>
                <li>The <strong>Submitted by</strong> value is selected from active users.</li>
                <li>A form version can be attached to a workflow definition.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="submissions" class="mf-panel">
            <h2>Submissions</h2>
            <p>Submissions are completed intake forms. Each submission receives a stable number and can be reviewed, routed, converted, and linked back to operational records.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Status values include <code>Submitted</code>, <code>In Review</code>, <code>Approved</code>, <code>Returned</code>, and <code>Converted</code>.</li>
                <li><strong>My Submissions</strong> shows the current user's work.</li>
                <li><strong>All Submissions</strong> is permission-gated.</li>
                <li>Conversion is locked until a submission is approved.</li>
                <li>Conversion can reuse a matching existing client or create a new client.</li>
                <li>Converted submissions link back to the client and matter records.</li>
                <li>Dates and times display in the user's local timezone when the browser provides timezone information.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="attachments" class="mf-panel">
            <h2>Attachments</h2>
            <p>Submissions can carry small supporting files and links so intake materials stay with the request instead of wandering through email.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>File uploads currently allow PDF, Word, and Excel-style files up to 25 MB.</li>
                <li>Hyperlink attachments can be added when the supporting material already lives somewhere else.</li>
                <li>Uploaded files are stored in Azure Blob Storage when cloud storage is configured.</li>
                <li>Attachment links appear on submission details and are permission-gated with submission access.</li>
                <li>Demo mode blocks destructive/admin-style behavior where appropriate.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="workflows" class="mf-panel">
            <h2>Workflows</h2>
            <p>Workflows define approval and notification steps. A workflow can be scoped to a form or used as a fallback, and a published form version can point to a workflow definition.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Workflow steps are ordered by step number.</li>
                <li>Step type can be <code>Approval</code> or <code>Notification</code>.</li>
                <li>Steps can be assigned to a user, a team, or both.</li>
                <li>Workflow queues include personal, team, and all-open views based on permissions.</li>
                <li>Outcomes can advance, complete, or return a workflow.</li>
                <li>Answer-based routing can run steps only when a submitted field matches a condition.</li>
                <li>Notification steps resolve recipients from <code>assigned</code>, <code>submitter</code>, or literal email addresses, then continue automatically to the next matching step.</li>
                <li>Notification templates support <code>{{SubmissionNumber}}</code>, <code>{{SubmissionStatus}}</code>, <code>{{SubmitterName}}</code>, <code>{{WorkflowName}}</code>, and <code>{{StepName}}</code>.</li>
                <li>If outbound email is disabled or incomplete, notification steps write a workflow event instead of blocking the request.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mb-0"><strong>Outcome format:</strong> use <code>Label|Action|Status|Next step</code>, separated by semicolons. Example: <code>Approve|Advance|In Review; Return|Return|Returned</code>.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="entities" class="mf-panel">
            <h2>Entities</h2>
            <p>Entities are operational records used by the platform after intake is approved or data is imported.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Clients</strong> have client numbers, contact details, status, and related matters.</li>
                <li><strong>Matters</strong> have matter numbers, client links, status, practice area, responsible user, related parties, and related contacts.</li>
                <li><strong>Parties</strong> support conflicts searching through names, aliases, relationships, and matter roles.</li>
                <li><strong>Contacts</strong> are address-book records that can link to clients and matters without becoming login users or conflict parties.</li>
                <li><strong>Users</strong> are application data users with system IDs, name parts, email, title, activity status, teams, and roles.</li>
                <li>Matter details show related contacts, parties, conflict searches, and recent time entries.</li>
                <li>Clients, matters, parties, and users can be archived from the front end so inactive records stop cluttering the main lists while remaining searchable and restorable.</li>
                <li>Client, matter, and party detail pages include conversation-style notes so users can leave follow-up comments directly on the record.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="conflicts" class="mf-panel">
            <h2>Conflicts</h2>
            <p>Conflict searches compare entered search terms against parties, party aliases, clients, client aliases, matter names, and prior conflict history. The search engine normalizes names, scores fuzzy matches, expands strong party matches through known relationships, and checks previous search/review text for recurring issues.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Searches can be linked to a submission or a matter.</li>
                <li>Results show match strength, risk, matched item, matter/client context, explanation, AI assist assessment, and row-level clearance state.</li>
                <li>Reviewer decisions can be recorded at the overall search level or on individual result rows.</li>
                <li>Individual or selected result rows can be escalated to another active user for approval with escalation notes. The assigned reviewer can approve the escalation with approval notes, and both actions are written to audit history.</li>
                <li>Previous conflict search names, terms, review notes, AI summaries, and result clearance notes are included as searchable history.</li>
                <li>Submission forms can show a floating live conflict preview while a user types client and matter names, giving early warnings before the form is submitted.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>How Search Terms Work</h3>
            <p>Enter the names or phrases you want checked. Each line, comma, or semicolon becomes its own search term, so one conflict search can check several parties at once.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Separate terms:</strong> <code>Acme Anvil Works, Swindon Logistics; Mina Caldera</code> is treated as three terms.</li>
                <li><strong>Cleanup:</strong> searches ignore case, punctuation, accents, and common company suffixes such as <code>Inc.</code>, <code>LLC</code>, <code>Ltd.</code>, <code>Group</code>, and <code>Holdings</code>. Connectors such as <code>&amp;</code>, <code>+</code>, and <code>and</code> are treated alike.</li>
                <li><strong>Initials:</strong> dotted initials stay together, so <code>A.A.W.</code> is searched like <code>AAW</code>, not as three unrelated letters.</li>
                <li><strong>Aliases count:</strong> CMIForge checks party names, party aliases, client names, and client aliases.</li>
                <li><strong>Matters count:</strong> matter names are searched directly, and results show matter, client, and party-role context when available.</li>
                <li><strong>Strong matches expand:</strong> when a term strongly matches a party, related parties can appear as lower-scored relationship results.</li>
                <li><strong>History counts:</strong> prior search text and prior clearance notes can appear when they are close enough to the term.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="table-responsive mt-3">
                <table class="table align-middle mf-compact-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Typed term</th>
                            <th>How CMIForge reads it</th>
                            <th>What that means</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>Acme Anvil Works, Inc.</code></td>
                            <td><code>acme anvil works</code></td>
                            <td>The company suffix is ignored, so this can match <code>Acme Anvil Works</code>, <code>Acme Anvils</code>, or a close alias.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>A.A.W.</code></td>
                            <td><code>aaw</code></td>
                            <td>Initials are preserved as one compact term, so it should match an <code>A.A.W.</code> alias without randomly matching a word that merely contains one letter.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>Stark &amp; Stone Legal</code></td>
                            <td><code>stark and stone legal</code></td>
                            <td>Ampersands and written connectors are treated alike, so spacing or <code>&amp;</code> versus <code>and</code> does not block a match.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>Elm &amp; Vine</code></td>
                            <td><code>elm and vine</code></td>
                            <td>Connectors normalize together, so this can match names such as <code>Elm and Vine Capital</code> or <code>Elm &amp; Vine Manufacturing</code>.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>Mina Q. Caldera</code></td>
                            <td><code>mina q caldera</code></td>
                            <td>Middle initials stay searchable, while close aliases like <code>Mina Caldera</code> can still score as fuzzy matches.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <h3>Reading Match Strength</h3>
            <p>Match strength is a similarity score from 0 to 100. It is not the final legal conflicts decision; it is a triage signal that helps reviewers decide what deserves attention.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Exact normalized match:</strong> the cleaned-up term and cleaned-up party or alias are the same.</li>
                <li><strong>Strong phrase match:</strong> one cleaned-up phrase contains the other, and both sides are long enough to be meaningful.</li>
                <li><strong>Strong fuzzy match:</strong> the names are similar by token, spelling, or trigram comparison even when they are not exact.</li>
                <li><strong>Token/contains match:</strong> the result is weaker but still close enough to show as a safety-net hit.</li>
                <li><strong>Risk level:</strong> risk also considers context such as adverse-party roles, linked matters, relationships, and prior history, so two results with similar scores can have different risk labels.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mb-0"><strong>Current review behavior:</strong> use row-level clearance to document how each hit was resolved. Result decisions roll up into the overall search status when they collectively indicate clear, needs-info, potential-conflict, or conflict outcomes. Use escalation when another firm user needs to approve or weigh in on a specific hit before the search is finalized.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="imports" class="mf-panel">
            <h2>Imports</h2>
            <p>The Import Center loads CSV files for clients, matters, and parties. Each import card includes a downloadable template, validation mode, and import mode.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Validate CSV</strong> checks the file and stores row-level messages without creating or updating records.</li>
                <li><strong>Import CSV</strong> runs the same checks and writes the accepted rows.</li>
                <li>Each import type includes a downloadable CSV template.</li>
                <li>Clients upsert by <code>ClientNumber</code>.</li>
                <li>Matters upsert by <code>MatterNumber</code> and require an existing <code>ClientNumber</code>.</li>
                <li>Parties link to matters by <code>MatterNumber</code> and reuse existing parties by normalized name.</li>
                <li>Client photo OCR can read a client image or scan, show the extracted text for review, and draft the client fields before creation.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="time" class="mf-panel">
            <h2>Time</h2>
            <p>Time recording is matter-centered. It is intentionally operational for now, not a full billing system.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Time entries receive stable eight-digit numbers.</li>
                <li>Each entry links to a user, client, and matter.</li>
                <li>Entries capture work date, hours, billable flag, status, client narrative, internal notes, and optional phase/task codes.</li>
                <li>Status values include <code>Draft</code>, <code>Submitted</code>, and <code>Approved</code>.</li>
                <li>Matters can require lead-partner approval before submitted time becomes approved.</li>
                <li>Approved and exported entries are locked from normal editing.</li>
                <li>The time list can be filtered by date range, matter, status, and user when permissions allow.</li>
                <li>Users can export filtered time entries to CSV.</li>
                <li>Matter pages provide a shortcut to record time directly against that matter.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="reports" class="mf-panel">
            <h2>Reports</h2>
            <p>Reports include built-in operational views plus a basic report builder for simple ad hoc questions.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Report access is controlled by the reporting permission and plan feature gates.</li>
                <li>Built-in reports include intake pipeline, approval queue aging, conflicts review, matter roster, and time detail.</li>
                <li>The basic report builder supports selectable datasets, selectable fields, up to three lightweight filters, and CSV export.</li>
                <li>Available builder datasets include submissions, workflow tasks, conflicts, matters, time entries, clients, and contacts.</li>
                <li>Visible results are capped to keep the page responsive, with CSV export available for spreadsheet review.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="security" class="mf-panel">
            <h2>Security</h2>
            <p>CMIForge has an internal role and permission model. Authentication is optional during local development, but permissions still control what the current user can see and do.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Users can have direct roles.</li>
                <li>Teams can have roles.</li>
                <li>Users inherit permissions from their teams.</li>
                <li>Navigation and server-side page handlers both enforce permissions.</li>
                <li>Optional Microsoft Entra login can map authenticated people to rows in the <code>Users</code> table.</li>
                <li>Authorized admins can use Security &gt; Impersonation to temporarily view CMIForge as another active user for support, workflow routing, and approval testing.</li>
                <li>Impersonation shows a visible banner and can be stopped from the banner or Security area.</li>
                <li>Demo mode protects system user <code>00000001</code> and blocks destructive demo actions.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="audit" class="mf-panel">
            <h2>Audit</h2>
            <p>The audit log records sensitive or administrative actions so the platform can explain who changed important records and when.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Audited actions include administrative/security changes, conversion, attachment changes, settings updates, and destructive actions as those actions are added.</li>
                <li>Audit entries capture actor details, action, entity type, entity identifier, summary, IP address, user agent, and optional JSON details.</li>
                <li>When impersonation is active, audit entries still keep the actual signed-in user as the actor and include the impersonated user in the details.</li>
                <li>The Security area includes an audit log view for users with the right permissions.</li>
                <li>Audit timestamps render through the same user-local timezone helper used elsewhere in the app.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="settings" class="mf-panel">
            <h2>Settings & Terms</h2>
            <p>System settings hold operational configuration such as support contact details, default timezone fallback, and email notification settings.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Settings are grouped by category, starting with General and Email.</li>
                <li>SMTP password configuration is represented as a secret reference so the actual secret can live outside the settings table.</li>
                <li>Settings updates are audit logged.</li>
                <li>Settings are hidden from the System menu and read-only in demo mode.</li>
                <li>Password resets, MFA, account verification, lockout, and sign-in policy belong in Microsoft Entra, not inside CMIForge settings.</li>
                <li>The Terms & Conditions page lives under System and describes the baseline SaaS terms, legal-use rules, license conditions, data protection posture, support access, maintenance access, and related customer responsibilities.</li>
                <li>Public workspace signup requires the submitter to accept the current CMIForge SaaS Terms, Legal Use, and License Agreement before the request can be sent.</li>
                <li>Agreement acceptance is recorded with the signup request, including the customer name, signer name and email, accepted agreement version, product version, timestamp, IP address, and browser user agent.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="demo-mode" class="mf-panel">
            <h2>Demo Mode</h2>
            <p>The live demo is intentionally separated from the prototype/dev database and is designed to be safe for public visitors.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>A visible banner explains that the environment is a public demo.</li>
                <li>The live demo uses the separate <code>cmiforge-demo</code> Azure SQL database.</li>
                <li>Demo data resets every 12 hours while the App Service process is running.</li>
                <li>The System menu includes a Demo Mode page with a manual reset button.</li>
                <li>Manual reset clears demo-created records and rebuilds the starter dataset.</li>
                <li>Administrative/designer changes and destructive actions are blocked in demo mode.</li>
                <li>User <code>00000001</code> is protected.</li>
                <li>Submitted text is screened for obvious abusive or unsafe demo content before records are created.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="plans" class="mf-panel">
            <h2>Plans</h2>
            <p>Plans are currently hardcoded product gates, not billing. They help test the product shape before payment infrastructure exists.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Community</strong>: free, 3 users, 50 matters, 50 clients, all features, and community support.</li>
                <li><strong>Professional</strong>: $149/month, includes 10 users, 500 matters, 500 clients, all features, and email support.</li>
                <li><strong>Enterprise</strong>: coming soon.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mb-0">The development and live demo build is currently hardcoded to Professional so development work and evaluation are not blocked by feature gates.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="current-limits" class="mf-panel">
            <h2>Current Limits And Next Slices</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Conflict result clearance now supports row-level status, notes, reviewer, and timestamp fields; richer clearance workflow templates are still a future slice.</li>
                <li>Conflict searches now search prior search text and previous result clearance notes; deeper full-text indexing is still a future scaling slice.</li>
                <li>Submission attachments now support small PDF, Word, Excel, and hyperlink records. Richer attachment workflows are still a future slice.</li>
                <li>Workflow notification steps are wired for SMTP-backed email and workflow-event logging. Tenant email delivery still depends on completed SMTP/secret configuration.</li>
                <li>Time recording is not yet billing, invoice generation, rate management, or accounting sync.</li>
                <li>Reporting now has built-in reports and a basic builder. Rich grouping, charts, scheduled delivery, and saved report definitions are future slices.</li>
                <li>Workflow versioning is not fully separated from workflow editing yet, so careful admin control matters.</li>
                <li>The current UI is functional and work-focused; richer designer screens are still a future slice.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
    </div>
</section>
